/**
 * `handleTaskMove`'s stale-resource cleanup must never force-delete a CUSTOM
 * branch.
 *
 * When worktree creation fails, task-move.ts runs a best-effort cleanup that
 * clears leftovers from a previous partial cleanup, ending in
 * `worktreeManager.removeBranch(expectedBranch)` - which is `git branch -D` with
 * every error swallowed. That is correct for the auto-generated branch it was
 * written for, and data loss for an agent-supplied one: ANY worktree failure,
 * while the branch is not checked out anywhere, silently destroys the branch and
 * its commits.
 *
 * Task #538 is the concrete case. Its branch carried commit 4261ff1e and had
 * never been pushed, so origin would not have saved it either. The only reason
 * this had not already bitten is that git refuses to delete a CHECKED-OUT
 * branch - the very condition that caused the failure - and the main checkout
 * has since moved off it.
 *
 * Both directions are pinned, because an over-correcting fix would strand stale
 * branches instead of deleting work.
 *
 * Red-green: the custom-branch test reds against the pre-fix handler, which
 * called removeBranch unconditionally.
 *
 * Mocks mirror abort-task-move.test.ts, with one change: WorktreeManager's
 * methods are HOISTED `vi.fn()`s rather than per-instance fields, because
 * task-move constructs `new WorktreeManager(...)` inside the catch block, so
 * per-instance spies are unreachable from the test.
 *
 * `isAutoGeneratedBranch` and `legacyAutoBranchNameFor` are deliberately left
 * UNMOCKED - the predicate is the thing under test, so stubbing it would make
 * this vacuous.
 */

import { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach } from 'vitest';
